body:
Today a caller searches through `SuffixArray`, which wraps `SufrFile<u32>` or `SufrFile<u64>`. Each operation takes its own options struct, and each struct carries the query list plus `low_memory` and `max_query_len`:

```rust
let mut sa = SuffixArray::read("x.sufr", true)?;
let res = sa.count(CountOptions { queries, max_query_len: None, low_memory: true })?;
```

`count`, `locate` and `extract` each rerun the search. The per-call `low_memory` and `max_query_len` decide which suffix array gets loaded into memory, and the first search loads it lazily.

## Proposal

Storage and the match length are fixed at open. A search takes only the query and returns a `Hits` value that borrows the array.

```rust
let sa = SuffixArray::open_with("x.sufr", OpenOptions {
    text: Load::Memory,
    suffix_array: Load::Memory,
    max_query_len: Some(20),
})?;
let hits = sa.search(b"ACG")?;
hits.count();
hits.locate()?;
hits.extract(Context { before: 10, after: Some(50) })?;
let all: Vec<Result<Hits>> = sa.search_many(&queries);
```

`list` leaves the library. The `sufr` CLI prints rows from `SuffixArray::suffixes` and `SuffixArray::lcps`.

## Cost

The public API breaks. `CountOptions`, `LocateOptions`, `ExtractOptions`, `SearchOptions`, `ListOptions` and their result types go away. `SufrFile::read` becomes `SufrFile::open`, which takes an `OpenOptions`. One open `SuffixArray` answers at a single `max_query_len`. A caller who wants two lengths opens the file twice.
